  "result": "I revised the record for `LVMC_11_40_180_A` and wrote it to `record.jsonl`. It has not been re-run through the gate.\n\nThe `forbid` witness failed because of how the run classifies electric scooters, not because of the rule. The run treats the \"electric scooter\" definition in LVMC 11.02.083 as unresolved. That definition has limits no data source can see: a weight of 100 pounds or less, a top motor speed of 20 mph or less, and no scooters built mainly to help a person with a disability get around. So a scooter's membership comes out as unknown, and the planned \"electric scooter on the sidewalk\" scene can never be judged a violation. Unknown is the correct result when those facts can't be seen.\n\nWhat I changed:\n- **Kept the scooter mapping.** Electric scooters still count under the definition, not the simpler scooter class (`ru.electric_scooter`). That class has none of the definition's limits, so using it would quietly drop them.\n- **Updated the a2 justification.** It now says outright that electric scooters come out unknown under the current classification.\n- **Swapped the `forbid` scenario's device to a bicycle.** It is now \"a bicycle moves along that sidewalk,\" which should give `violated`, since bicycles already get decided verdicts in the `permit` scenario.\n- **Left the rule itself unchanged.** The coder's rule should still fit the revised record; only the `forbid` witness changes, from an electric scooter to a bicycle.\n- **Removed the `p.halt_speed_tolerance` proposal.** The parameter is already in the catalogue, so the record now just names it.\n\n**Decision for you:** with the current classification, this rule can never find an electric scooter on the Fremont Street sidewalk in violation, even though the law names scooters. If you think scooters with handlebars and an electric motor should count, the classification has to change, as the coder suggested. I couldn't do that from this record.",
